q(1,'Explain why representation needs accountability to those represented.','Representatives make decisions affecting others; communication and scrutiny help link their choices to constituents’ needs rather than their presence alone.',['Reason: decisions on behalf of others linked to scrutiny.'])
q(2,'Suggest an accountability arrangement for an invented elected committee whose members do not report spending decisions to residents.','Publish usable records and hold an accessible reporting meeting with a route to question decisions. This addresses missing information and feedback.',['Context: unreported spending.','Reason: connect records and resident scrutiny.'])
q(10,'State two different features used to classify governments.','How the head of state is chosen, and how the executive is held accountable.',['Feature: two distinct political dimensions.'])
q(11,'Differentiate a monarchy from a republic on the head-of-state dimension.','A monarchy has a monarch, commonly hereditary; a republic has a non-hereditary public head-of-state office. Neither label alone establishes democracy.',['Comparison: paired head-of-state distinction.'])
q(18,'Differentiate capitalism and a mixed economy.','Capitalism emphasises private ownership and markets; a mixed economy explicitly combines private and public activity. The categories describe broad arrangements rather than a complete prediction of outcomes.',['Comparison: ownership/activity mix.'])
q(19,'Explain why an ownership label alone does not prove development success.','Access, resource use and accountability affect whether production benefits people. Either public or private activity can perform poorly when those conditions fail.',['Reason: ownership linked to outcome conditions rather than automatic benefit.'])
q(39,'Explain why private shops alongside a state-owned water provider indicate a mixed arrangement rather than exclusively private production.','Private and public enterprises coexist. Test ownership across the productive activities, rather than assuming markets or private shops exclude a public role.',['Error: private activity taken as proof of an exclusively private economy.','Distinction: private emphasis versus explicit coexistence.'])
for n,s in [(25,'04-00'),(27,'04-01'),(28,'04-02'),(29,'04-03'),(30,'04-04'),(31,'04-05'),(32,'05-00')]:q(n,'Explain one development mechanism concerning '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',' '.join(L[s]),['Reason: '+L[s][1]])
cases={26:('local service responsibility is transferred but funds and trained operators remain unavailable','The local body has responsibility without means to deliver. Resources and capacity must accompany decentralisation for service access to improve.'),33:('a funded workshop is built but no training or electricity connection is arranged','The separate missing supports prevent installed capacity becoming useful output; construction expenditure alone is not successful implementation.'),34:('a clinic is built far from intended users and no transport was planned','The location and missing connection restrict access despite the asset; planning needs actual users and operating access.'),35:('new machines arrive but staff cannot operate them and no training is funded','A skill gap leaves equipment idle; relevant instruction and support address that constraint more directly than additional machines.'),36:('repair funds are diverted while service faults remain unresolved','Diversion prevents maintenance being carried out and weakens reliable access; records and accountable action need to address the resource loss.'),37:('orders and workers exist but a small material purchase cannot be financed','The missing input blocks use of existing resources; suitable finance can enable the orders if repayment and demand remain viable.'),38:('procedures change but staff are neither trained nor given practice support','Staff may continue unusable old methods; relevant training and supervised application can help translate the change into functioning work.')}
for n,(case,a) in cases.items():q(n,'Explain the development mechanism in this fictional case: '+case+'.',a,['Mechanism: use both resource/implementation facts.','Consequence: '+a])
P={'P01':(['01-00','04-05'],'State (a) the meanings of representation and delegation [2]. Explain (b) why an invented transfer of water-service tasks without staff or funds may not improve local access [4].','(a) Acting for others; assigning specified responsibilities within limits. (b) Local responsibility can improve responsiveness, but absent staff and funding prevent operation. Actual means and accountability must accompany the transfer.',['Point: correct meanings [2].','Development: connect transferred tasks and missing means to the service outcome [4].']),
'P02':(['04-00','05-00'],'Describe (a) this fictional comparison: A spends N$100,000 on buildings and has 30% functioning service coverage; B spends N$80,000 with staffing and maintenance and has 70% [3]. Suggest (b) a priority for A where faults and unstaffed facilities are reported, aiming at usable access [3].','(a) Higher capital-building expenditure accompanies lower functioning coverage in A; B has coverage 40 points higher despite lower expenditure. (b) Address staffing and maintenance with clear responsibility and funding, then measure access. The pair does not isolate causes, but A’s stated faults identify practical priorities.',['Evidence: compare spending and coverage [3].','Meaning: match the response to documented faults and access, without assuming more buildings solve them [3].'])}
